.Dd October 20, 2026
.Dt KRAFT 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm kraft
.Nd coding agent
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl jprtwy
.Op Fl a Ar api
.Op Fl c Ar tokens
.Op Fl e Ar effort
.Op Fl m Ar model
.Op Fl n Ar max_tokens
.Op Fl P Ar mode
.Op Fl u Ar url
.Op Ar task ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a coding agent.
You describe a task; a language model works on it by asking
.Nm
to read and change files and run commands in the current directory,
and
.Nm
carries those requests out, asking you before anything that changes
the system.
The model keeps going until the task is done, then waits for the next.
.Pp
With a
.Ar task
on the command line,
.Nm
carries it out and exits.
Otherwise it prompts for tasks with
.Sq >\ \&
until end of input, with line editing and history, and a line above
the prompt showing the model, the permission mode, how full the
context window is and the tokens used so far.
A line ending in a backslash goes on to the next, for tasks of more
than one line.
When standard input is not a terminal, each line is a task.
.Pp
On a terminal, replies are shown with their Markdown rendered (bold,
headings, code in colour), tool calls and their results are marked
out, and a spinner shows while the model is thinking.
Colour is left out when output is not a terminal, or when
.Ev NO_COLOR
is set.
A word
.Ar @path
in a task attaches that file, if it is an image or (with the
Anthropic API) a PDF, for the model to see;
.Ar @server : Ns Ar uri
attaches the text of an MCP server's resource.
.Pp
The conversation is kept between tasks, so later tasks can refer to
earlier ones, and saved, so that
.Fl r
can resume it.
.Pp
In a git repository, the model is told the branch, the status and
the recent commits as they were at the start, and a task that changes
the uncommitted state ends with a summary of it.
.Pp
When the conversation grows to about three quarters of the model's
context window,
.Nm
has the model summarize it, and carries on with the summary in its
place.
It does the same, once, if the server refuses a request as too long.
A request that fails for a reason that may pass (no connection, a
busy or overloaded server, a reply cut short) is tried up to five
more times, after 2, 4, 8, 16 and 32 seconds, or as long as the
server asks.
With the Anthropic API, requests use prompt caching, so the
unchanging start of each request is billed at a fraction of the
usual price.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a Ar api
The API to speak:
.Cm anthropic
(the Anthropic Messages API,
.Pa /v1/messages ,
the default) or
.Cm openai
(an OpenAI-compatible Chat Completions API,
.Pa /v1/chat/completions ) .
.It Fl c Ar tokens
The size of the model's context window.
By default it is asked of the server: the Anthropic Models API
reports it, and so does LM Studio.
If neither answers,
.Nm
compacts only when a request is refused as too long.
.It Fl e Ar effort
How much effort the model should put into each reply:
.Cm low ,
.Cm medium ,
.Cm high ,
.Cm xhigh
or
.Cm max ;
more effort means more thinking, and more tokens.
With the Anthropic API it is sent as
.Ql output_config.effort ;
with an OpenAI-compatible one as
.Ql reasoning_effort ,
where
.Cm xhigh
and
.Cm max
become
.Cm high .
By default the server decides.
The auto-mode check always asks for
.Cm low .
.It Fl m Ar model
The model to use.
.It Fl n Ar max_tokens
The most tokens the model may generate in one reply.
The default is 16384.
.It Fl r
Resume the newest session started in the current directory,
carrying on the conversation where it left off.
Every session is saved as it goes; see
.Sx FILES .
.It Fl t
Copy the model's reasoning, when the server sends it, to standard
error.
With the Anthropic API, a summary of the reasoning is asked for,
since the reasoning itself is not sent.
.It Fl u Ar url
The base URL of the server, with or without a trailing
.Pa /v1 .
Both http and https are supported.
The default is
.Pa http://127.0.0.1:1234 .
.It Fl j
As
.Fl p ,
but what goes to standard output is a JSON object:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"result": "the final reply", "error": false, "status": 0,
 "model": "...", "requests": 3, "input_tokens": 12,
 "cache_read_tokens": 9000, "cache_write_tokens": 400,
 "output_tokens": 250, "cost_usd": 0.0123,
 "session": "/home/you/.kraft/sessions/..."}
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql cost_usd
is
.Ql null
when it cannot be known.
.It Fl p
For scripts: carry out the task given as arguments, or else all of
standard input as one task, then exit.
Only the final reply goes to standard output; tool calls, diffs and
the like go to standard error, without colour.
There being no terminal to ask on, calls that would need approval are
refused unless the permission mode allows them.
.It Fl P Ar mode
When tool calls that change things need your approval; see
.Sx PERMISSIONS .
The default is
.Cm ask .
.It Fl w
Let the model search the web, with the Anthropic API's own search
tool.
The searches run on Anthropic's servers, so they cannot be put to
you first; that is why this is not the default.
Only for
.Pa https://api.anthropic.com .
.It Fl y
The same as
.Fl P Cm yes .
.El
.Sh PERMISSIONS
Reading and searching never need approval.
Writing and editing files and running commands do, depending on the
permission mode:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm ask
Every such call is shown and you are asked: answer
.Cm y
to allow the call,
.Cm n
to refuse it, or
.Cm a
to allow that tool for the rest of the session, or
.Cm p
to allow exactly this call from now on: a rule saying so is added to
the project's
.Pa .kraft/config
(see
.Sx CONFIGURATION ) .
.It Cm rules
Calls that a fixed, conservative set of rules finds safe are allowed
without asking; you are asked about the rest, with the reason.
A file may be written or edited if it is inside the current
directory, after resolving symbolic links and
.Pa .. ,
and not inside
.Pa .git .
A command may run if every command in it is a common read-only or
build tool (such as
.Cm ls ,
.Cm grep ,
.Cm git status ,
.Cm make
and
.Cm cc )
or a program in the current directory, and it uses no command or
process substitution, here-document, or redirection other than to
.Pa /dev/null .
Anything else, such as
.Cm rm ,
.Cm make install ,
.Cm sed -i
or
.Cm find -delete ,
is asked about.
.It Cm auto
As
.Cm rules ,
but when the rules cannot settle a call, the model is asked, in a
separate request with no tools, whether the call is safe and part
of what you asked for.
It sees your recent requests and the call, but no tool output, so
text in the files it read cannot argue for the call.
If it answers
.Cm ALLOW ,
the call runs and its reason is shown; otherwise you are asked.
.Pp
Some calls are always put to you, whatever the model says: running
anything as another user
.Pf ( Cm sudo ,
.Cm doas ,
.Cm su ) ,
.Cm rm
with
.Fl r
or
.Fl f ,
.Cm git push
and
.Cm git clean ,
recursive
.Cm chmod
and
.Cm chown ,
piping into a shell,
.Cm dd ,
.Cm newfs
and the like, and writing outside the current directory or in
.Pa .git .
.It Cm yes
Every call is allowed without asking.
Use this only where the model can do no harm, such as a scratch jail.
.El
.Pp
Without a terminal to ask on, calls that would be asked about are
refused.
.Pp
Whatever the mode, the model cannot use the tools to read or change
anything in
.Pa ~/.kraft ,
where the API key and saved sessions live, and the API key is taken
out of the environment before any command runs.
Commands also run with limits: an hour of CPU time per process, files
of at most 4 GB, at most 2048 processes for the user, and no core
dumps.
.Pp
Rules in the config files come before the mode: a
.Cm deny
rule refuses a call in every mode,
.Cm yes
included, and an
.Cm allow
rule lets one run without asking.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
Settings are read from
.Pa ~/.kraft/config ,
then from
.Pa .kraft/config
in the current directory; environment variables and flags override
them.
Each line is a key and a value; blank lines and lines starting with
.Ql #
are ignored.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
url https://api.anthropic.com
model claude-opus-5
mode auto
allow bash make test*
allow edit src/*
deny bash git push*
.Ed
.Pp
The settings are
.Cm api ,
.Cm url ,
.Cm model ,
.Cm check_model ,
.Cm effort ,
.Cm mode ,
.Cm max_tokens ,
.Cm context ,
.Cm web_search
and
.Cm show_thinking
(the last two
.Cm yes
or
.Cm no ) ,
as for the options and environment variables of the same meaning.
.Pp
.Cm allow Ar tool pattern
and
.Cm deny Ar tool pattern
are permission rules.
.Ar tool
is a tool's name (or a glob matching names),
.Cm edit
for
.Cm write_file ,
.Cm edit_file
and
.Cm multi_edit ,
or
.Cm * .
.Ar pattern
is a shell glob, in which
.Ql *
matches anything,
.Ql /
included; it is matched against the command for
.Cm bash ,
the path, relative to the current directory, for the file tools, and
the URL for
.Cm web_fetch .
An
.Cm allow
rule for
.Cm bash
matches only a plain command, with none of
.Ql ;\& & | $( \` < >
in it, so that
.Ql make test*
does not also allow
.Ql make test; rm -rf ~ .
A
.Cm deny
rule for
.Cm bash
also matches any part of a compound command.
No rule can allow changing a
.Pa .kraft
directory.
.Pp
Hooks are commands, run with
.Pa /bin/sh
in the current directory, around the model's tool calls:
.Bl -tag -width "hook before tool command"
.It Cm hook before Ar tool command
runs before each call of a tool that
.Ar tool
matches (as for rules), once it is allowed.
If it fails, the call is not made, and what it printed goes to the
model as the reason.
.It Cm hook after Ar tool command
runs after each such call; what it prints is added to the result the
model sees.
.It Cm hook done Ar command
runs when a task is over, and what it prints is shown.
.El
.Pp
The call is described to them in the environment:
.Ev KRAFT_TOOL ,
.Ev KRAFT_INPUT
(its JSON),
.Ev KRAFT_PATH
and
.Ev KRAFT_COMMAND
(if it has a path or a command),
and, after it,
.Ev KRAFT_RESULT
and
.Ev KRAFT_FAILED
(1 if it failed; for
.Cm done ,
if the task did).
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hook after edit case "$KRAFT_PATH" in *.c) clang-format -i "$KRAFT_PATH";; esac
hook before bash ! echo "$KRAFT_COMMAND" | grep -q sudo
hook done printf '\ea'
.Ed
.Pp
A project's
.Pa .kraft/config
is used only once you have trusted it: kraft shows it and asks the
first time, and again whenever it changes, since a project from
elsewhere could otherwise give itself any permission.
Rules that kraft adds itself, with the
.Cm p
answer, keep it trusted.
.Pp
.Cm mcp Ar name command
adds a Model Context Protocol server: a command, run with
.Pa /bin/sh ,
that speaks MCP on its standard input and output.
It is started with
.Nm ,
and its tools are offered to the model as
.Cm mcp__ Ns Ar name Ns Cm __ Ns Ar tool .
A project's config may name a server of the same name as yours, and
replaces it.
For example:
If
.Ar command
is an
.Li http
or
.Li https
URL, the server is reached there instead, on MCP's streamable HTTP
transport.
.Cm mcp_header Ar name header
adds a request header for it, after the
.Cm mcp
line; in it,
.Li ${ Ns Ar VAR Ns Li }
is replaced by the environment variable
.Ar VAR ,
so that a token need not be written in the file.
A project's config that names a server of yours does not get your
headers for it.
OAuth is not supported; a server that needs it may accept a token
made by hand.
For example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mcp fs npx -y @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem /home/me/notes
mcp gh https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/
mcp_header gh Authorization: Bearer ${GITHUB_TOKEN}
.Ed
.Pp
As there is no knowing what another program's tools do, every call
is subject to the permission mode; a rule such as
.Ql allow mcp__fs__* *
allows a server's tools without asking.
.Pp
A server's prompts become commands,
.Cm / Ns Ar name : Ns Ar prompt Op Ar arguments :
the arguments, separated by spaces, fill the prompt's in order, the
last taking the rest of the line, and the prompt the server makes of
them is shown and run as a task.
Its resources can be attached to a task with
.Ar @name : Ns Ar uri ,
and read by the model with
.Cm read_resource .
.Sh TOOLS
The model may call these tools; those marked with an asterisk are
subject to the permission mode.
Calls in one reply are made in order, except that a run of calls
that change nothing
.Pf ( Cm read_file ,
.Cm grep ,
.Cm find_files ,
.Cm list_dir
and
.Cm symbols )
or start sub-agents
.Pf ( Cm task )
is made all at once, each in a process of its own.
Sub-agents working at the same time take turns to ask you anything,
and cannot start background commands; an
.Cm a
answer given to one lasts only as long as it does.
.Bl -tag -width "bash_output"
.It Cm read_file
Read a file, with line numbers.
An image (PNG, JPEG, GIF or WebP) is shown to the model as an image.
A PDF is sent as a document to the Anthropic API; elsewhere its text
is read with
.Xr pdftotext 1 ,
if
.Pa graphics/poppler-utils
is installed.
.It Cm write_file Ns *
Create or replace a file.
.It Cm edit_file Ns *
Replace text in a file.
The text must match exactly and, unless all occurrences are to be
replaced, occur exactly once.
.It Cm multi_edit Ns *
Make several such replacements in one file, all or none.
.It Cm bash Ns *
Run a command with
.Pa /bin/sh ,
or start it in the background.
.It Cm bash_output
Read a background command's new output, waiting for some if need be.
.It Cm bash_kill
Stop a background command.
Background commands are stopped when
.Nm
exits.
.It Cm find_files
Find files by name.
.It Cm grep
Search file contents with
.Xr grep 1 .
.It Cm list_dir
List a directory.
.It Cm symbols
Find where functions, types, macros and the like are defined, by name
or glob, kind and path, or outline a file, with
.Xr uctags 1 .
Offered only if
.Pa devel/universal-ctags
is installed.
.It Cm read_resource
Read an MCP server's resource, as text.
Offered only if a server has resources; some of them are named in the
tool's description.
.It Cm web_fetch Ns *
Fetch an http or https URL and return its text, HTML turned into
plain text.
.It Cm task
Hand a self-contained piece of work to a sub-agent: a separate
conversation with the same tools, less
.Cm task ,
.Cm ask_user
and
.Cm todo ,
whose calls are shown indented and are subject to the permission
mode.
Only its final report comes back, so what it read does not fill the
conversation.
Several started in one reply work at the same time.
.It Cm todo
Keep a checklist for a task of several steps, shown as it changes.
.It Cm ask_user
Ask you a question, with possible answers to choose from, and wait
for the answer.
.It Cm web_search
Search the web.
Offered only with
.Fl w
on the Anthropic API.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
At the prompt:
.Bl -tag -width "/compact"
.It Cm /clear
Forget the conversation.
.It Cm / Ns Ar name Op Ar arguments
Run a command of your own: the prompt in
.Pa .kraft/commands/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .md
in the project or, failing that,
.Pa ~/.kraft/commands/ Ns Ar name Ns Pa .md ,
with
.Ql $ARGUMENTS
in it replaced by the
.Ar arguments
(or the arguments added at the end, if it has no
.Ql $ARGUMENTS ) .
The first line of the file describes it, for
.Cm /help .
A project's command is shown before it runs, since it came with the
project.
.It Cm /init
Have the model look around the project and write a
.Pa KRAFT.md
for it, or improve the one there is; the writing is subject to the
permission mode.
.It Cm /remember Oo Fl g Oc Ar text
Add
.Ar text
as a line to the project's
.Pa KRAFT.md ,
or with
.Fl g
to
.Pa ~/.kraft/KRAFT.md ,
and tell the model now.
.It Cm /compact
Replace the conversation with a summary of it, to save space.
.It Cm /undo
Put back the files that the last task with file changes wrote or
edited, and remove those it created; again, to go further back.
Only changes made with
.Cm write_file ,
.Cm edit_file
and
.Cm multi_edit
can be undone, not what commands did.
.It Cm /rules
List the permission rules in force, and where each came from.
.It Cm /mcp
List the MCP servers, whether each is running, and their tools,
prompts and resources.
.It Cm /diff
Show the uncommitted changes, in a git repository.
.It Cm /commit
Have the model look at the uncommitted changes and commit them, with
a message in the style of the repository's recent ones.
It does not push.
The commit is a command like any other, subject to the permission
mode.
.It Cm /usage
Show the tokens used this session: input, how much of it was read
from or written to the prompt cache, output, and roughly how full
the context window is; and, with the Anthropic API, what the session
has cost at Anthropic's list prices (which kraft knows only for the
models current when it was released, and which may since have
changed).
The status line shows the cost too.
.It Cm /model Op Ar model
Show or change the model.
Unless the context window was given, it is looked up afresh, and the
auto-mode check follows the change unless it has a model of its own.
With the Anthropic API a change makes the next request rebuild the
prompt cache.
.It Cm /effort Op Ar effort
Show or change the effort, as for
.Fl e ,
or
.Cm default
to leave it to the server.
With the Anthropic API a change makes the next request rebuild the
prompt cache.
.It Cm /mode Op Cm ask | rules | auto | yes
Show or change the permission mode.
The prompt shows it:
.Sq auto>\ \&
or
.Sq yes>\ \&
instead of
.Sq >\ \& .
.It Cm /help
List the commands.
.It Cm /exit
Quit.
So does end of input (control-D).
.El
.Pp
Control-C stops the reply or command in progress and returns to the
prompt; the text received so far is kept.
A second control-C kills a command that ignores the first.
At the prompt, control-C clears the line.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
.It Ev KRAFT_API
Default for
.Fl a .
.It Ev KRAFT_MODEL
Default for
.Fl m .
.It Ev KRAFT_EFFORT
Default for
.Fl e .
.It Ev KRAFT_CONTEXT
Default for
.Fl c .
.It Ev KRAFT_MODE
Default for
.Fl P .
.It Ev KRAFT_CHECK_MODEL
The model for the
.Cm auto
mode check; by default the main model.
It must be served by the same server, at the same URL.
.It Ev KRAFT_URL
Default for
.Fl u .
.It Ev NO_COLOR
If set, no colour or other terminal attributes are used.
.It Ev KRAFT_API_KEY
The key to send.
If unset,
.Ev ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
or
.Ev OPENAI_API_KEY
is used, depending on the API, and failing that the key file.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "~/.kraft/anthropic.key"
.It Pa ~/.kraft/anthropic.key , ~/.kraft/openai.key
The API key, used when none is set in the environment.
The file may hold the bare key or a line such as
.Ql export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=... ;
it is read, never run.
It should be readable only by you.
.It Pa ~/.kraft/sessions/
Saved conversations, one file per session, rewritten after every
step and readable only by you.
They hold everything the model saw, file contents included; remove
old ones when they are no longer wanted.
Beside each is a
.Pa .undo
directory holding the files as they were before each change, for
.Cm /undo .
.It Pa ~/.kraft/config , .kraft/config
Settings and permission rules; see
.Sx CONFIGURATION .
.It Pa ~/.kraft/trusted
The project config files you have trusted, with a hash of each.
.It Pa ~/.kraft/mcp- Ns Ar name Ns Pa .log
The standard error of MCP server
.Ar name ,
from its last start.
.It Pa ~/.kraft/KRAFT.md
Instructions for every project, given to the model first.
.It Pa KRAFT.md
Project instructions, given to the model: those in the current
directory and in each directory above it, up to the top of the git
repository (or to just below
.Ev HOME ) ,
the outermost first.
.Cm /init
writes one;
.Cm /remember
adds to one.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 100 on a permanent failure such as bad usage or
a rejected request, 111 on a temporary failure such as a network
error (after the retries), and 130 if a task given on the command
line was interrupted.
These are meant to be relied on by scripts.
.Pp
To have kraft do something unattended and report as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kraft -j -P auto 'run the tests and fix any failures' > out.json
.Ed
.Sh EXAMPLES
Work with a model served by LM Studio on another machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kraft -u http://192.168.10.234:1234 -m qwen/qwen3.8-27b
.Ed
.Pp
Have Claude fix a failing build, then exit:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kraft -u https://api.anthropic.com -m claude-opus-5 \e
    'make fails; find out why and fix it'
.Ed
.Pp
Pick up that conversation again later, in the same directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kraft -r -u https://api.anthropic.com -m claude-opus-5
.Ed
.Sh BUGS
In
.Cm auto
mode each call the rules cannot settle costs one extra request, and
a small model's judgement can be erratic; it errs towards asking.
.Pp
The auto-mode rules judge commands by their text alone; a program
on the list can still be made to do harm through a project's own
files, such as a hostile
.Pa Makefile .
Use
.Cm rules
and
.Cm auto
on code you trust.
